{
  "title": "Re-architect calorie estimates: separate ingredient recognition from \"calories for a known ingredient\"",
  "description": "The calorie feature (all built since 2026-09) has grown far beyond what it does: about 3.9 MB of embedded catalog JSON (curated.json is 334 KB, mostly stored LLM classification output, including ~250 junk parser-leftover keys like \"-ounce block feta\"), a ~1,200-line nutrition engine with regex special cases (ramekin-core/src/nutrition/, catalog/mod.rs resolve_line), two near-duplicate LLM work queues (server/src/ingredient_names.rs and ingredient_weights.rs) each with pending/resolved/failed/harvested states plus reask flags, candidate re-asks and model re-asks, three version/invalidation schemes (calories-v17 database_version that no client reads, catalog-v5 + content hash, categorizer_version on every shopping item), a harvest loop that writes LLM answers back into curated.json, and a 1,450-line audit CLI. \
Owner direction: split it cleanly into two parts. (1) Ingredient recognition/normalization: turn a parsed line's item text into a canonical food (or \"not food\" / \"unknown\"). \
This is the hard part and can stay complicated (catalog lookup, aliases, LLM fallback). (2) Calories for a known ingredient and amount: given a canonical food, unit and amount, return grams and kcal. \
This should be close to trivial: a table lookup plus unit conversion, with no LLM calls, queues or version schemes of its own. \
Start with a design write-up for the owner: what each part owns, what state is stored (ideally one queue for unknown names, failures retried automatically, no automatic re-asks), which of today's mechanisms go away (likely: reask_other_models, candidate re-asks, harvest-into-curated, database_version, the per-item categorizer_version touch on every catalog edit), and how the junk aliases get regenerated or dropped. \
Get the design approved before the rewrite. \
Known problems the redesign should fix: any catalog alias edit changes candidate lists and triggers paid re-asks on deploy; any catalog edit touches every live shopping item at startup so every client resyncs its whole list; queue_stored re-estimates every recipe in the database after each resolved batch; estimate-calories queues AI work for arbitrary unsaved ingredient lists with no per-user limit. \
Expect make pipeline data/ diffs.",
